WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.640
Today's video is about subdividing&nbsp;
triangles and possibly indications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.180
of how such subdivisions happen&nbsp;
in higher dimensional analogues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:16.320
of triangles. So we start with a triangle&nbsp;
and we use a subdivision that is classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.640
in mathematics, the Barycentric subdivision.
- (Brady: This could be any&nbsp;triangle, basically?)

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:26.820
This could be any triangle, but for the time being&nbsp;
let's let's be as an equilateral triangle. We want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:32.220
to subdivide the area of this triangle into&nbsp;
smaller pieces. The barycentric subdivision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:38.580
proceeds as follows: in order to subdivide it&nbsp;
we need more vertices first. And the vertices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:46.560
here we get by looking at the so-called faces of&nbsp;
the object. So faces are the vertices, the edges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:53.220
and the whole triangle. And for each of those&nbsp;
we take the barycentre; which is like the centre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:57.960
point in a in a certain sense, the sum of the&nbsp;
vertices divided by the number of vertices;&nbsp;

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:06.120
and for the actual vertices this is just the&nbsp;
vertex itself; for an edge it's the midpoint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:12.060
of this edge; and then we have the barycentre&nbsp;
of the whole triangle somewhere here. And now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:18.180
we need to know how to draw the edges of the&nbsp;
new triangle, and we need a rule for that. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:27.420
the rule is that we connect two vertices of this&nbsp;
new set of vertices if the corresponding faces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:35.640
are contained in each other. So for example, we&nbsp;
keep this edge from here to here - so this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:42.120
now a single edge - because this is the centre&nbsp;
point of this edge which contains this vertex.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:49.560
We also have an edge from here to here because&nbsp;
this vertex is part of the whole triangle.&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:57.060
We have no edge from here to here because&nbsp;
this vertex is not contained in this edge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:03.360
so we continue with that so this one is we have&nbsp;
an edge here because this edge is contained in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.460
the whole triangle and so on. So this is the&nbsp;
barycentric subdivision of this triangle;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:16.200
we've got six new triangles. You can continue,&nbsp;
you can now uh start subdividing again to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:23.100
new refinement like- now you go to each of those&nbsp;
triangles, you do the same thing, subdivide, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:32.880
can do this to each of these six. Again subdivide&nbsp;
and you get a finer and finer set of triangles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:40.020
which covers the original area of these triangles.
- (You were telling me there&nbsp;might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.080
be a problem with this.)
- Well it depends on what purpose you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:50.520
doing that for. I mean one purpose you could do is&nbsp;
an applied purpose, you have many triangles that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:58.680
say, try to approximate a shape - you model some&nbsp;
monster in a movie. Your original set of triangles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:03.480
is too coarse, there are too many edges in your&nbsp;
monster and you want to have it more smooth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:10.080
and then you try to do this by having smaller&nbsp;
and smaller triangles. Now if you do barycentric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:15.960
subdivision one of the disadvantages of this&nbsp;
subdivision is that the angles in these triangles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:24.060
they become more and more acute or obtuse. So&nbsp;
here you have a very acute triangle and here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:30.480
even more and so on. So the angles become uneven,&nbsp;
the triangles tend to be kind of lengthy, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.940
that's not what you want usually if you want to&nbsp;
model. For mathematical purposes the barycentric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:40.080
subdivision is perfectly fine, there are very&nbsp;
many applications in mathematics of barycentric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:44.580
subdivisions of triangles, higher dimensional&nbsp;
simplices, and so on. The alternative is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:52.560
so-called Edgewise Subdivision. So now, again, we&nbsp;
need first to create a new set of vertices and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:58.920
way we create this new set of vertices here is&nbsp;
a different one. So I will draw a little second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:04.860
picture, which now is supposed to be in 3-space.&nbsp;
These are the coordinate axes of 3-dimensional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:11.760
space, and now I place exactly this triangle&nbsp;
here in this position. So the coordinates now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:18.180
of these three points are 1 in the x Direction,&nbsp;
0 in the y direction and 0 in the z direction;&nbsp;

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:26.040
and here I get 0 in the x direction, 1 in&nbsp;
the y direction, 0 in this z direction and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:33.300
then here have 001. And now I choose a number&nbsp;
r and I dilate this triangle by this number r.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:45.060
So let's, for example, take r equals to 3. This&nbsp;
point now is 300, this point here is somewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:53.760
here 030, this point here now being 003. On&nbsp;
this new triangle there are no more points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.760 --> 00:05:00.720
with integer coordinates. On this line here&nbsp;
we also have the point with coordinates 120&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:07.440
