which has coordinates 111. So this gives us&nbsp;
our new set of points that in the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:22.260
barycentric subdivision we had obtained by taking&nbsp;
barycentres. So let me draw these points now here;&nbsp;

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:28.380
so now for r equals to 3, so the number we have&nbsp;
chosen, is a kind of degree of freedom for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:38.340
subdivision. And now this vertex becomes like&nbsp;
300, this one is 030, and this 003. So this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:42.900
these correspondents between the triangle right&nbsp;
here and the triangle I've drawn here. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:48.360
we draw the additional vertices: these are these&nbsp;
integer points here from these lines copied,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:57.780
and then the point in the middle 111. Okay, now&nbsp;
again we need a rule how to connect these points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:02.760
in order to form the&nbsp;new triangles.
- (Why do we need a rule? how come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:05.280
I- I) (can't just do it by&nbsp;
eye? Like it's not- you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:09.780
I couldn't just join all the dots?)
- Yeah, actually in- if you if you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:16.440
to create a very even subdivision then prob-&nbsp;
there's a good guess; and the good guess is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:21.360
this one is a triangle, this one here is a&nbsp;
triangle, this is a triangle and so on. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

